den and slightly Pete  Budaj edge who has been climbing as Joe  Biden continues to falter this has been  a very solid week for Elizabeth Warren  she's now more solidly into the double  digits averaging about  eleven percent in the national polls and  as I said a month ago Elizabeth Warren  had been picking up on average one to  one-and-a-half percentage points per  month if she continues this into the  debates she could and the key word is  could could be in a position to actually  overtake Bernie Sanders and later on in  the program today I'm going to address  an audience question which wants to know  whether Bernie and Warren are  potentially going to sort of just split  the progressive vote and maybe hurt the  progressive movement in the long term  I'll talk about that later on in the  program the question for Elizabeth  Warren as the debates start will be is  she going to be better prepared for  these debates than she has in Prior  circumstances and maybe preparations not  the right word she's just not a great  debater right I love Elizabeth Warren's  policy focused town halls  I love the detailed campaign policy  proposals that she has released  I love the campaign she's running but I  recognize that on a debate stage with I  don't know how they're if it's going to  be nine candidates on each of the two  they're splitting the first two debates  into two groups because there are so  many Democratic candidates I don't know  if Elizabeth Warren on a stage with  eight other people is going to do well  in a way that is actually going to help  her polling because I just don't think  that's the best sort of setting for her  so this is national polling let's look  at a couple of other things including  Iowa because meanwhile in Iowa Joe Biden  is now barely leading at all Biden is  holding an average lead of just five  points in Iowa over Bernie Sanders with  a margin of error somewhere between  three and four typically that is not  particularly good for Joe Biden so I  believe that Iowa is effectively up for  grabs I don't mean Biden is losing as of  today what I mean is it's close enough  and far away and it's the the polling is  close enough and the caucus is far away  enough that the debates could actually  push Iowa to Bernie Sanders or even  potentially to somebody else depending  on how the debates go looking at New  Hampshire not surprisingly New Hampshire  has a different sort of  a theme New Hampshire Democrats are more  establishment centrist  than Iowa caucus-goers maybe and Joe  Biden has a larger lead there he's plus  13 in New Hampshire Iowa is the first  caucus the New Hampshire primary comes  after that so there are a couple of  different views on these polls and on  the 2020 primary one view is it's over  if you look at June of the year before  the election historically the leader in  June of the year before the election  usually ends up being the nominee  meaning its Biden and that's it that's  one view  ok the other view is this thing is  totally wide-open anyone can win this  and it's still early I think neither of  you is is probably the right one here it  is not wide open but there is still  certainly a chance for one of the other  frontrunners to win historically there  have been moments in June of the year  before the election where you have one  candidate polling 60% and then a bunch  of other people in the single digits  that is not the situation this year you  have Joe Biden dropping and only leading  Bernie Sanders by a much more modest  amount right now and you have multiple  candidates in double digits certainly  now Biden Sanders and Warren and that  could even change with Budaj edge or  maybe even Harris as the debates get  going so I do think that there is an  opportunity here for it to be somebody  other than Joe Biden I know that there  are some people emailing me who say  things like David nothing that has  happened in the past matters this time  around because everything is different  Trump as president as the status quo is  so different than anything that has  happened before the leftward swing of  Democratic voters is like nothing that  we've ever seen before  every election this happens every  election there's a contingent that says  nothing from the past matters because  this time it's so different I think that  these are new circumstances but anybody  who's polling under 1% is done ok I know  people are emailing me even saying that  people like Beto are about to surge  drastically as soon as the debates start  I don't think so and he's polling like 3  or 4 percent  but if you're pulling under 1% I mean  just it the dynamics are not so  different this year that you know I get  emails from people saying Tulsi is going  to so dominate the debates that she'll  be leading after the second debate  no she won't okay Beto is not all of a  sudden going to take the lead he should  go back to Texas and run against Senator  Cornyn there I think he's got a better  shot it is possible for someone from the  top three the top four maybe the top  five who could pull into the lead and  ultimately take this nomination  historically its most likely to be  Bernie or Biden okay given where the  polls are now given how these primaries  have gone basically every time in  history it's going to be Biden or Bernie  most likely and there is like a 99%  certainty that it will be Biden Sanders  Warren Budaj edge or Harris okay if  you're outside of that group I don't see  how you ultimately take this nomination  now that doesn't mean that you shouldn't  support someone outside of that group if  that's who you want to see when I  believe that a diverse primary is a good  thing I believe that you should support  whoever is most aligned with your values  and on the issues what I am telling you  in terms of a historical analysis is  that it is extraordinarily unlikely that  anyone outside of the current top five  ultimately is the nominee if you  disagree with me tell me either how am i  misunderstanding the current data or how  is the historical context not relevant  to what is going on this particular time  let me know I'm on Twitter at D Pakman  the show is on twitter at david pakman  show and it's a good one it's a good one  this week today's sponsor is Skillshare  and I'm excited to tell you that  everybody in our audience is getting two  entire months of free access to  Skillshare when you click the link  underneath this video skill shares the  site that offers over 25,000 online  courses on almost any topic you can  imagine cooking meditation photography  video editing Photoshop managing a small  business almost an endless list and  whether you're trying to get ahead in  your career maybe you want to learn new  skills that will make  you better at your job you might just be  exploring a new hobby and you're trying  to find new ways to be creative in your  personal time skill shares the perfect  place to fully immerse yourself in  whatever you're trying to learn I really  enjoyed the class on more advanced video  editing techniques because it's  something I've been wanting to improve  at but have been very busy hosting the  david packard show for the last few  years  once again the david pakman show is  giving you two whole months of access to  every single class offered by Skillshare  after those two months if you want to  keep your subscription Skillshare super  affordable at less than ten bucks a  month get better at whatever it is that  you're passionate about or discover  something brand new that you didn't even  know that you would love and join skill  shares other 7 million users by clicking  the link underneath this video  [Music]

